events.content:
Lazy loading confirmed: `React.lazy(() => import('./renderer/YagrWidget'))` — the engine code is only fetched when that type renders. 

One catch: `gravity-charts/index.js` does `export { ChartTooltipContent, CustomShapeRenderer } from '@gravity-ui/charts';` — this statically imports from @gravity-ui/charts at module top level! Hmm, but with bundlers, `export {X} from` — vite/rollup will include @gravity-ui/charts code in the chunk that imports the plugin... unless tree-shaking removes unused re-exports. Since I only import `GravityChartsPlugin`, rollup can tree-shake the re-export IF the module has no side effects. That might partially break laziness of the gravity-charts engine. Hmm. But the plugin module is `import {GravityChartsPlugin} from '@gravity-ui/chartkit/gravity-charts'` — if I import this statically in main.tsx, rollup may statically include @gravity-ui/charts exports (ChartTooltipContent etc.) — that pulls part of charts into the main bundle.

To be safe and guarantee laziness, I can lazy-load the plugin registration itself: register plugins via dynamic import in the app entry:

```ts
const loadPlugins = Promise.all([
  import('@gravity-ui/chartkit/gravity-charts'),
  import('@gravity-ui/chartkit/yagr'),
]).then(([gc, yagr]) => settings.set({plugins: [gc.GravityChartsPlugin, yagr.YagrPlugin]}));
```

Hmm, but the ChartKit component would need plugins registered before render. Actually a cleaner approach: register plugins in main.tsx statically (this is the documented pattern) — the plugin module itself is tiny; the renderer (which imports the actual engine) is React.lazy. The `export {ChartTooltipContent, CustomShapeRenderer} from '@gravity-ui/charts'` re-export could pull charts code eagerly depending on bundler tree-shaking. With Vite/Rollup, re-exports of unused bindings from a side-effect-free module are tree-shaken... Actually `export {X} from './other'` creates a dependency — Rollup will include './other' module in the graph, and if @gravity-ui/charts has side effects... package.json sideEffects flag matters.

Alternative robust approach: don't import plugin modules at entry at all. Instead, define my own plugin objects wrapping dynamic imports... but the docs pattern is to use the provided plugin objects. Hmm, but I could also create my own plugins:

```ts
const GravityChartsLazyPlugin = {
  type: 'gravity-charts',
  renderer: React.lazy(() => import('@gravity-ui/chartkit/gravity-charts').then(...))
}
```

No — renderer must be a component; the plugin's renderer is YagrWidget/GravityChartsWidget component, not re-exported separately... Let me check if the renderer components are importable directly: `@gravity-ui/chartkit/build/plugins/gravity-charts/renderer/GravityChartsWidget`. Not in exports map, so no.

The documented and idiomatic pattern: `settings.set({plugins: [GravityChartsPlugin, YagrPlugin]})` at entry, with plugin modules imported statically. The heavy engines load via React.lazy dynamic imports. The re-export in gravity-charts/index.js is a minor leak that Rollup tree-shaking may handle — let me just check charts package.json sideEffects.

Actually, simpler: to make engine loading truly lazy AND keep the "single widget API" requirement, I can register plugins asynchronously inside the widget module itself via dynamic import. Pattern:

```tsx
// chartPlugins.ts
import {settings} from '@gravity-ui/chartkit';

export async function ensurePluginsRegistered() { ... }
```

But ChartKit throws if type not registered at render. So the widget would need to wait for registration before rendering ChartKit. That complicates things.

Honestly, the documented pattern is: static import of the plugin registry modules at entry, React.lazy renderers handle engine laziness. The re-export concern: let me check `@gravity-ui/charts` package.json sideEffects field. If sideEffects: false, rollup tree-shakes unused re-exports and nothing from charts gets bundled eagerly.

Let me check.

